\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[node distance=1.0cm]
\node[sys box,text width=5.7cm,minimum height=1.25cm] (st) at (-3.4,0) {\textbf{Paper statement}\\Label, wording and hypotheses};
\node[sys box,text width=5.7cm,minimum height=1.25cm] (pa) at (3.4,0) {\textbf{Explanatory passage}\\Meaning, scope and attribution};
\node[sys box,text width=5.7cm,minimum height=1.7cm] (su) at (-3.4,-2.5) {\textbf{Registered formal supports}\\Pinned declarations; support status;\\row-level relation note};
\node[sys box,text width=5.7cm,minimum height=1.7cm] (so) at (3.4,-2.5) {\textbf{Cited source excerpts}\\Files and line ranges; hashes;\\authored justification};
\draw[sys arrow] (st)--node[sys label,right,text width=2.8cm] {resolve recorded\\statement links} (su);
\draw[sys arrow] (pa)--node[sys label,right,text width=2.8cm] {check recorded\\text and sources} (so);
\node[sys box,text width=12.5cm,minimum height=1.1cm] (rv) at (0,-4.8) {\textbf{Reviewer compares the account with its evidence}\\Does the explanation preserve the conditions, conclusion and credit?};
\draw[sys arrow] (su.south)--(rv.north -| su.south);
\draw[sys arrow] (so.south)--(rv.north -| so.south);
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{Two records, with different checking tasks. Arrows show which information is inspected. They do not assert logical implication or automatic approval. Formal acceptance does not settle whether the explanatory passage is faithful to the result.}
\label{fig:checks}
\end{figure}

From a complete public checkout, the document checks are:
\begin{quote}\small
\texttt{python3 scripts/systems\_paper\_evidence.py}\\
\texttt{python3 scripts/build\_systems\_paper\_counts.py}
\end{quote}
The first checks passage bindings and source hashes. The second checks the generated inventory against its records. A selected writing packet supports only the checks for which it includes the required files; selected Lean excerpts are not a complete proof environment. Proof replay and formal comparison require their own recorded revisions, dependencies and toolchains.
